    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Japanese crossed the strait where the Australians held an 11 mile (18 km) front, and the defenders, spread along the whole coast with no suitable reserve, could not hold the beachhead.</li><li>Yamashita later called his attack a bluff, saying he had 30,000 men, was outnumbered more than three to one and feared being forced into costly street fighting, while Percival's garrison was short of water, food and ammunition.</li><li>Churchill called the fall of Singapore the worst disaster and largest capitulation in British history, and the loss of the base severely undermined British prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the main attack the Japanese fired 88,000 shells along the strait in 15 hours, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> Command did not take the bombardment as a prelude to a landing and ordered no counter-battery fire on the Japanese assembly areas.</p></aside>
    </section>

