    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1923</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> begins to establish a naval base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> after the Anglo-Japanese alliance lapses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1941</span><span class="rf-text">The Japanese 25th Army invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and Japanese aircraft sink the battleship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and the battlecruiser Repulse</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1942</span><span class="rf-text">The last Commonwealth forces cross the causeway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> on 31 January and engineers blow it up</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1942</span><span class="rf-text">On 8 February Japanese troops begin crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> Strait and land against the 22nd Australian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1942</span><span class="rf-text">The Jurong Line collapses on 10 February and the Japanese take the Bukit Timah area the next day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1942</span><span class="rf-text">On 15 February Percival surrenders to Yamashita at the Ford Motor Factory</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> renames the city Syonan-to and murders thousands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching massacre</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1946</span><span class="rf-text">Yamashita is hanged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> after a war crimes trial that did not cover crimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="fall-of-singapore" data-v="d405d5732b" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The island divided into three sectors</b> Two Australian brigades held the west, where the invasion was most likely, III Indian Corps held the north-east and the Southern Area forces the south-east. The Japanese stood across the Johor Strait.</li><li><b>The 5th and 18th Divisions land in the north-west</b> The first wave crossed the strait shortly before 20:30. The main weight, about 13,000 men, fell on the 22nd Australian Brigade, which numbered just 3,000.</li><li><b>The 22nd Brigade falls back towards the centre</b> The Australian battalions that had been engaged regrouped and moved back east from the coast, and the Japanese divisions followed inland. The Australians were pushed back further in the early morning.</li><li><b>The Imperial Guards attack the 27th Brigade</b> The Guards crossed on the 27th Brigade's front, near the causeway, and some reached the shore. Mortars, machine-guns and burning oil on the water caused them severe casualties, and Yamashita ordered them to press on.</li><li><b>The 27th Brigade withdraws from the causeway</b> Maxwell could not contact the 22nd Brigade and feared encirclement. His brigade withdrew east of the Bukit Timah Road, and the Allies lost the beaches by the causeway. Bennett later said he had not authorised the move.</li><li><b>The Japanese push on to Bukit Panjang and Bukit Timah</b> The 5th Division aimed at Bukit Panjang and the 18th Division at Bukit Timah. The 18th fell on the 22nd Brigade's area, and the Japanese captured the Bukit Timah area with the garrison's main food and fuel depots.</li><li><b>The Commonwealth forces pull back to a city perimeter</b> The Commonwealth forces withdrew from the northern shore and Changi to a perimeter around Singapore City at the eastern end of the island, with harassing attacks all the way. The 44th Brigade held from Buona Vista to Pasir Panjang.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built a naval base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> after the Anglo-Japanese alliance lapsed in 1923, and the base became a key part of interwar defence planning for the region under the Singapore strategy. Financial constraints hampered construction, and by the time war broke out in the Pacific shifting circumstances had largely undermined the premises of the strategy. During 1940 and 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> imposed a trade embargo on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in response to its campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and its occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>. Japanese oil reserves were being rapidly depleted, and in the latter half of 1941 Japan began preparing for war to seize vital resources if peaceful efforts to buy them failed.</p><p>The basic plan for taking Singapore was worked out in July 1940. When the German raider Atlantis captured the steamer Automedon in November 1940, its papers, which described the weakness of the Singapore base, were handed to the Japanese in December. The Japanese had also broken British Army codes, and in January 1941 they read a message from Singapore to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> complaining of the weak state of Fortress Singapore. They first suspected a plant and accepted it only after checking it against the Automedon papers.</p><p>The Japanese 25th Army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> from Indochina on 8 December 1941, almost at the same time as the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>. British thinking held the Japanese to be inferior and the jungle to be impassable, and the Japanese repeatedly outflanked hasty defensive lines. On 31 January 1942 the last Commonwealth forces crossed the causeway to Singapore and engineers blew it up.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Singapore&#8221; cites where it covers Outbreak of war, Invasion of Malaya, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Chris Coulthard-Clark, The Encyclopaedia of Australia's Battles (Allen and Unwin, 2001)</li><li>Timothy Hall, The Fall of Singapore 1942 (Methuen, 1983)</li><li>Martin Brayley, The British Army 1939–45: The Far East (Osprey, 2002)</li><li>Peter Dennis, The Oxford Companion to Australian Military History (Oxford University Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Percival commanded 85,000 men, the equivalent on paper of just over four divisions, of whom 15,000 were employed in supply, administrative or other non-combatant roles. The infantry numbered 49 battalions: 21 Indian, 13 British, six Australian, four Indian States Forces, three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> Volunteer Force and two Malayan. The newly arrived 18th Infantry Division was at full strength but lacked experience and training. The rest of the force was of mixed quality, and the Australian official historian Lionel Wigmore noted that only one Indian battalion was up to numerical strength. Losses on the mainland had also left a general shortage of equipment.</p><p>Yamashita had just over 30,000 men from three divisions, the Imperial Guards, the 5th and the 18th, with a light tank brigade in support. The Japanese were superior in close air support, armour, co-ordination, tactics and experience, and had gained air supremacy; by the invasion only ten Hurricanes remained at Kallang.</p><p>Percival gave Major-General Gordon Bennett's 8th Australian Division the west of the island, including the likely landing points, with a frontage of over 11 mi (18 km) and no serious fixed defences, while the freshest formation, the 18th Division, held the north-east. Percival guessed that the Japanese would land in the north-east, ignoring advice that the north-west was more likely, and the Japanese deliberately moved troops to encourage this. He had spread his forces thinly along the whole coast and kept no suitable reserve.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 3 February the Japanese shelled the garrison, and air attacks intensified over five days. Shortly before 20:30 on 8 February the first wave of the 5th and 18th Divisions began crossing the strait. The main weight, about 13,000 men from 16 assault battalions, fell on the 22nd Australian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, whose battalions numbered about 3,000 men with no significant reserve. During the first night 13,000 Japanese landed, and another 10,000 followed after first light. Darkness and vegetation let the Japanese bypass or surround Australian pockets, and by 01:00 they were well established.</p><p>On 9 February Percival sent reinforcements, the Imperial Guards attacked the 27th Australian Brigade, and the 27th withdrew from its position, a move Bennett said he had not authorised. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> lost the beaches west of the causeway, and Guards armour and artillery crossed unopposed. On 10 February the Jurong Line collapsed after commanders misread orders and withdrew, and on 11 February the Japanese destroyed two thirds of X Battalion, a hastily formed unit of reinforcements, and took the Bukit Timah area with the garrison's main food and fuel depots.</p><p>Yamashita called on Percival on 11 February to give up. On the night of 12 and 13 February the Allies fell back to a 28 mi (45 km) perimeter round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> City, where a million civilians crowded into a small area as bombing, shelling and fears for the water supply grew. On 15 February Percival held a conference at 09:30, judged a counter-attack impossible and surrendered shortly after 17:15.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Singapore&#8221; cites where it covers Japanese landings, Air operations, Second day, Japanese breakthrough:</p><ul><li>Timothy Hall, The Fall of Singapore 1942 (Methuen, 1983)</li><li>Peter Dennis, The Oxford Companion to Australian Military History (Oxford University Press, 2008)</li><li>Louis Allen, Singapore 1941–1942 (Routledge, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sook-ching.jpg" alt="Sook Ching" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civilian_War_Memorial_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deoma12, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the terms hostilities ceased at 20:30, and Yamashita accepted full responsibility for the lives of the civilians. Nearly 85,000 British, Indian and Commonwealth troops were captured, in addition to losses earlier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, and about 5,000 men were killed or wounded, a majority of them Australian. Japanese casualties on the island were 1,713 to 1,714 killed and 2,772 to 3,378 wounded. About 7,000 civilians had died by 15 February, not counting hundreds killed in earlier raids. Bennett handed over his division and left by boat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, which caused controversy.</p><p>On 14 February Japanese troops entered the Alexandra Barracks Hospital and killed up to 50 soldiers, some of them undergoing surgery, along with doctors and nurses. About 200 staff and patients who had been bound were marched to an industrial area, bayoneted if they fell, held overnight in small rooms without water and bayoneted the next morning; a few survived by pretending to be dead. Later the Japanese murdered thousands of civilians, mostly ethnic Chinese, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching massacre</a>, and other communities were not spared.</p><p>The city was renamed Syonan-to. Prisoners were held at Changi, and thousands were shipped to forced labour on projects such as the Siam-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> Death Railway and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> airfield, many dying on the ships and in captivity. Of about 40,000 Indian personnel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, about 30,000 joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>, and about 7,000 fought in Burma, Kohima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imphal">Imphal</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Fall of Singapore&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Subsequent events:</p><ul><li>Jean Abshire, The History of Singapore (ABC-CLIO, 2011)</li><li>Kevin Blackburn, Did Singapore Have to Fall? Churchill and the Impregnable Fortress (Routledge, 2004)</li><li>Timothy Hall, The Fall of Singapore 1942 (Methuen, 1983)</li><li>Alan Warren, Britain's Greatest Defeat: Singapore 1942 (Hambeldon Continuum, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Australian official historian Lionel Wigmore described the state of the garrison's battalions, and a survivor of the Alexandra Hospital killings, Private Arthur Haines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> Regiment, wrote a four-page account that his daughter sold at auction in 2008. Yamashita's own remark that the attack was a bluff, Lord Moran's account of Churchill's reaction and Churchill's cable to Wavell of 10 February also survive; the cable put Percival's force at over 100,000 men, against 85,000 for the garrison's strength.</p><p>Numbers differ between accounts: the prisoners taken on the island are put at about 80,000 or nearly 85,000, and Japanese losses there at 1,713 to 1,714 dead and 2,772 to 3,378 wounded. The total for the campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> is 130,000 Commonwealth prisoners against 9,824 Japanese casualties.</p><p>The causes of defeat are disputed. Bennett blamed Percival and the Indian troops, and a classified wartime report by Wavell released in 1992 blamed the Australians. John Coates called that report lacking in substance and argued that the real cause was the failure of the Singapore strategy. The Kappe Report found that at most two thirds of the Australian troops manned the final perimeter. Clifford Kinvig blamed Brigadier Maxwell, and Costello called Percival's concentration of the 22nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> at the water's edge a serious mistake. Post-war analysis holds that a counter-attack on 15 February might have succeeded.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-curtin.jpg" alt="John Curtin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin">John Curtin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohnCurtin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Library of Australia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Churchill called the fall of Singapore the worst disaster and largest capitulation in British history. His physician Lord Moran wrote that it stupefied him and left a scar on his mind. Together with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and the loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and the Repulse, it severely undermined British prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Alan Warren writes that it shattered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>'s illusion of permanence and strength, and presaged the end of colonialism in the region after the war.</p><p>The Australian prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin">John Curtin</a>, compared the loss to the evacuation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> and said that the fall of Singapore opened the Battle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Hitler is reported to have forbidden his foreign minister to issue a congratulatory communique, deeming the defeat a setback for the white race.</p><p>Commando raids against shipping in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> Harbour followed in Operation Jaywick in 1943 and Operation Rimau in 1944, and British, Indian and Australian forces re-occupied the island in September 1945 in Operation Tiderace. Yamashita was convicted by a US military commission and hanged on 23 February 1946, but not for the crimes of his troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> or Singapore. Since 1998 Singapore has observed Total Defence Day on 15 February, the anniversary of the surrender, and sirens have sounded at 6.20 p.m. since 2015.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In February 1942 Japanese troops under Yamashita crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> Strait and, in a week of fighting, forced the surrender of Percival's much larger garrison on 15 February. About 80,000 men became prisoners, and Churchill called it the worst disaster in British military history.</em></p>

